{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
    // `lib` and `types` are here as a CONSEQUENCE of the `paths` block below,
    // not as a preference. Putting `packages/spec/src` into this program means
    // tsc now checks spec's own source files, which are written against spec's
    // own environment (`packages/spec/tsconfig.json`: `lib` ES + DOM +
    // DOM.Iterable, `types` node). Without them the first run reported two
    // `TS2591: Cannot find name 'process'` in `spec/src/shared/lazy-schema.ts`
    // — a verdict about THIS package's compiler environment, not about spec's
    // backward compatibility. Mirroring spec's own environment keeps every red
    // this gate produces attributable to the spec contract, which is the only
    // thing it is allowed to be read as.
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "strict": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    // `rootDir: "."` was removed for the same reason. It emits nothing under
    // `noEmit`, but it is still enforced: with spec source in the program it
    // produced 247 x `TS6059: File '.../packages/spec/src/...' is not under
    // 'rootDir'`, drowning the one error the gate exists to print.
    //
    // -- Why `paths` at all (#8021) ----------------------------------------
    //
    // This package is the repo's backward-compatibility gate for
    // `@objectstack/spec` (#2035), and its README gives typecheck a job the
    // suite next door cannot do: "a removed or NARROWED export fails here".
    // It declared no `paths`, so under NodeNext tsc resolved
    // `@objectstack/spec` through the workspace link and the `exports` map --
    // i.e. `dist/index.d.mts`, A BUILD ARTIFACT. The type half was therefore
    // rendering a verdict about the last `pnpm build`, exactly as the runtime
    // half was before #7991 aliased vitest to source.
    //
    // Measured on this package, direction predicted before running, one
    // variable moved -- identical checkout, identical stale `dist`
    // (`.build-input-hash cf8635aa...`), a narrowing injected into SOURCE only
    // and no rebuild (`spec/src/integration/connector.zod.ts`, `label:
    // z.string()` narrowed to `z.number()`, which the frozen `DcConnector`
    // fixture assigns `'DC HubSpot'` to):
    //
    //   without this `paths` block   tsc --noEmit -> exit 0, CLEAN
    //   with this `paths` block      src/additional-domains.fixtures.ts(35,3):
    //                                error TS2322: Type 'string' is not
    //                                assignable to type 'number'.
    //
    // Turbo orders `typecheck` after `^build`, so `turbo run typecheck` was
    // never the failing path and needs no change. What breaks is every path
    // turbo does not mediate: `pnpm --filter ... typecheck` in the package, an
    // editor's TS server, or an agent in a tree built at an older commit --
    // i.e. exactly the paths this gate is re-read on WHILE someone is changing
    // the spec, when it most needs to be telling the truth.
    //
    // -- The shape of the two rules ----------------------------------------
    //
    // ONE subpath rule for every namespace, never an enumeration: the fixtures
    // reach `automation` / `data` / `identity` / `integration` / `security` /
    // `system` / `ui` today, and a hand-maintained list goes stale silently the
    // first time a fixture reaches a new one -- silently, because the failure
    // mode is a green typecheck.
    //
    // The anchoring discipline #7991 records for Vite's ALIAS applies here in
    // a different spelling, so it is worth stating exactly. A tsconfig `paths`
    // key WITHOUT a `*` is an EXACT match, so the bare `@objectstack/spec`
    // entry cannot swallow `@objectstack/spec/ui` the way a Vite object-form
    // alias does (that trap is `ENOTDIR` at run time; see `vitest.config.ts`).
    // The tsconfig-shaped version of the same mistake is spelling the key
    // `@objectstack/spec*` -- star NOT preceded by a slash -- which matches
    // every namespace and folds it onto whatever the target names. That one is
    // WORSE than the Vite trap, because `spec/src/index.ts` re-exports most of
    // the namespace surface, so it does not crash: it type-checks the fixtures
    // against the wrong module and stays green. The pin cases in
    // `test/source-resolution.pin.test.ts` refuse that spelling by simulating
    // this block's resolution.
    //
    // Both rules are load-bearing and they fail INDEPENDENTLY -- measured,
    // with the subpath rule left in place and only the bare rule deleted, tsc
    // was still CLEAN while `src/stack.ts`'s `defineStack` types came from
    // `dist`. A missing rule here does not announce itself; that is what the
    // pin cases are for.
    "paths": {
      "@objectstack/spec": ["../../spec/src/index.ts"],
      "@objectstack/spec/*": ["../../spec/src/*/index.ts"]
    }
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*", "test/**/*"]
}
